    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nPressing `cgn` (\"change next match\") enters insert mode on all cursor positions but before that it only (visually) deletes the next matching selection for the primary cursor and also only updates the position of the primary cursor (to be on the match position). However after exiting insert mode all cursor positions seem to be updated correctly with the `gn`-matches correctly deleted and all newly inserted characters in the correct place.\nMust be some display update bug.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n- create several lines of text that all contain the same test string anywhere in the line  \n- `/`-search for the test string\n- place multiple cursors on the start of each line\n- press `cgn` on all cursor positions => only the primary cursors has the correct position and the match is only (visually) deleted here\n- press `Esc` to enter normal mode => everything is displayed correctly\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nConsistent behavior for all cursors.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-dev-1658+gd1fd8158de\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\ndoes not apply\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nArch Linux\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nkitty\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-kitty\n\n### Installation\n\nAUR",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "`gn` internally is implemented as search (`/`), which multicursor normally does not follow (absolute jump), however when the insert-mode edit resolves, it cascades correctly.\n\nThis is a cosmetic issue, worth looking at eventually. But it's a bit weird to use `cgn` with multicursor, because as mentioned it is \"shared navigation\": what happens if there are 3 cursors but only 1 match?",
